  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Unit: YM-000026 (<verbatim|manuscript/existence.tex>,
    <verbatim|manuscript/existence/*.tex>)

    <item>Role: mathematical validity, restricted to one question: does the
    #477 diff preserve the mathematics? This is not a whole-argument review.

    <item>Human reviewer: none. ADB991 has not reviewed this report. It was
    prepared by a scheduled agent acting under ADB991's account.

    <item>Agent assistance: Claude (Anthropic), session
    <verbatim|ADB991-claude-20261116-81c1>, wrote the scripts, read the
    residual hunks and wrote this report. No subagents were used.

    <item>Content inspected: PR #477, merged as <verbatim|962ac43b> with
    first-parent base <verbatim|9ded0b7a>. The
    <verbatim|manuscript/existence> tree is <verbatim|d422b163> after the PR
    and <verbatim|40777beb> before it. <verbatim|manuscript/existence.tex> is
    unchanged by the PR.

    <item>Source revision: Lean <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>,
    unchanged.

    <item>Review task and PR: #479 (claim
    <hlink|https://github.com/hairer/humanise-yang-mills/issues/479#issuecomment-6932410501|https://github.com/hairer/humanise-yang-mills/issues/479#issuecomment-6932410501>);
    the report PR is linked from the issue.

    <item>Outcome: no changed mathematical statement, constant or exponent
    was found. The PR's renames preserve the content of every hunk that
    touches math mode. Changes are requested at the level of notation and
    editing:

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>S1\US3: symbol overloads introduced by the renames, and an
      incomplete dilation conversion;

      <item>N1\UN3: old terms left undefined after their definitions were
      renamed, duplicated words, and one garbled sentence.
    </itemize-minus>
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Method and evidence><label|method-and-evidence>

  Every command below was actually run, on copies of the two trees made with
  <verbatim|git archive 9ded0b7a manuscript> and <verbatim|git archive
  962ac43b manuscript>.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Token-level substitution map.> Each changed <verbatim|.tex>
    file (180 files) was tokenised into control sequences, words, numbers,
    whitespace and single characters, and the two versions were aligned with
    <verbatim|difflib.SequenceMatcher> (<verbatim|autojunk=False>). The
    script is in the Bots note. Result: 525 distinct old\<rightarrow\>new
    substitutions, 3953 occurrences in total. The most frequent are
    <verbatim|price>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|bound> (1273),
    <verbatim|prices>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|bounds> (511),
    <verbatim|door>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|interface> (121),
    <verbatim|train>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|kernel history> (60) and
    <verbatim|bill>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|bound> (54).

    <item><strong|Label, reference and citation sets.> The multisets of
    <verbatim|\\label>, <verbatim|\\ref>, <verbatim|\\eqref> and
    <verbatim|\\cite> keys were compared across both trees.

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item><verbatim|\\eqref> (1305) and <verbatim|\\cite> (33) are
      identical.

      <item>One label was added, <verbatim|sec:ex-rg-terminology>
      (argument-outline.tex:11).

      <item>Seven <verbatim|\\ref>s were added: three to
      <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-trace-coffer-supplier>, which replace prose
      "the Coffer \<ldots\>", and one each to
      <verbatim|sec:ex-rg-terminology>, <verbatim|sec:ex-soft-curvature-receipts>,
      <verbatim|sec:heading-history-handoff> and
      <verbatim|sec:selected-zero-coordinate-coupon>.

      <item>All added references resolve to existing labels.

      <item>No key was removed.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|Hunks that touch math.> I flagged every aligned change
    whose old or new text contains a digit, one of <verbatim|^ _ \<less\>
    \<gtr\> = + / \|>, a binary minus, or one of <verbatim|\\le \\ge \\frac
    \\sum \\prod \\exp \\log \\sqrt \\min \\max \\sup \\inf \\int \\cdot
    \\times>. That gave 108 hunks, and I read each with its context. The
    verifications are listed under "Checked and found consistent".

    <item><strong|Collisions.> For every new math symbol or operator name I
    counted occurrences per file before and after the PR. A new symbol that
    already occurred in the same file is a candidate collision; I read each
    candidate.

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>These new operator names occur nowhere in the old tree, so they
      cannot collide: <verbatim|fieldCut>, <verbatim|fineCut>,
      <verbatim|zoomCap>, <verbatim|invDil>, <verbatim|stepSmall>,
      <verbatim|vacuumBound>, <verbatim|volFac>, <verbatim|rootFac>,
      <verbatim|redBud>, <verbatim|scaleDecay>, <verbatim|gradeDecay>,
      <verbatim|frameWeight>, <verbatim|symProd>, <verbatim|comparisonConst>,
      <verbatim|forcedReserve>, <verbatim|activityBound>,
      <verbatim|localizationBound>, <verbatim|pathCutoff>,
      <verbatim|pathBound>, <verbatim|countertermBound>,
      <verbatim|landMoment>, <verbatim|massiveSymbol>, <verbatim|probeBound>,
      <verbatim|quadBound>, <verbatim|scAllow>, <verbatim|stopBound>,
      <verbatim|logScale>, <verbatim|scaleRate>.

      <item>These subscripted families are new and fresh: <verbatim|\\mathcal
      B_{\\rm \<ldots\>}> (<verbatim|final>, <verbatim|loop>,
      <verbatim|merge>, <verbatim|row>, <verbatim|refl>, <verbatim|degree>,
      <verbatim|test>, <verbatim|read>, <verbatim|err>), <verbatim|\\mathfrak
      M> and <verbatim|R_{\\rm supp}>.

      <item>Reused letters are dealt with in S1 and S2.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|Merged names.> I built the inverse map (new name
    \<rightarrow\> set of old names) and checked each case in which two
    distinct old identifiers became one new identifier.

    <item><strong|Leftovers.> I counted whole-word occurrences of old
    identifiers in the new tree and read every file that now uses both the
    old and the new name.

    <item><strong|Newly duplicated words.> I searched both trees for a word
    repeated across whitespace, including line breaks, and kept the cases
    that are new in the PR.
  </enumerate>

  Structural checks on the review branch: <verbatim|python3
  scripts/check_blueprint.py> and <verbatim|python3 -m unittest discover -s
  tests> (results in the PR).

  <section|Checked and found consistent><label|checked-and-found-consistent>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|<verbatim|puffs> \<rightarrow\> explicit power.>

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>Old definition: <verbatim|\\operatorname{puffs}(H_i)=L_i^{1/8}>
      (actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex:303). This agrees with Lean
      <verbatim|YM.Bid.Loan.puffs L = L^(1/8)>
      (<verbatim|Lean/YM/Bid/Loan.lean:34>).

      <item>The replacements in centered-freight-producer.tex:142\U145 are
      <verbatim|L^{1/8}>, <verbatim|e=L^{-3/8}> (from <verbatim|puffs^{-3}>)
      and <verbatim|L^{-1/4}> (from <verbatim|puffs^{-2}>). The exponent
      arithmetic is correct.

      <item>In actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex the PR writes
      <verbatim|\\lambda_i> at l.303 and <verbatim|L_{N-1}^{1/8}> at l.496
      and l.531. These are consistent.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|<verbatim|momentPrice> \<rightarrow\> <verbatim|\\mathfrak
    M>.>

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>New definition at trace-local-charge.tex:470:
      <verbatim|\\mathfrak M(s,\\eta)=e^\\eta s!/\\eta^s>. This equals Lean
      <verbatim|momentPrice s \<eta\> := Real.exp \<eta\> * s.factorial /
      \<eta\>^s> (<verbatim|Lean/YM/Extra/ITERATION_NO_LOSS/Sums.lean:106>).

      <item>The stated bound <math|<around|(|1+g|)><rsup|s>*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*g>\<leqslant\>\<frak-M\><around|(|s,\<eta\>|)>>
      holds: put <math|x=1+g>; then <math|x<rsup|s>*e<rsup|-\<eta\>*x>\<leqslant\>s!/\<eta\><rsup|s>>
      by <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>*x>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<eta\>*x|)><rsup|s>/s!>.

      <item>trace-local-pairing.tex:855 and :870 rewrite
      <verbatim|momentPrice_P(a/4)> as <verbatim|\\mathfrak M(P,a/4)>. These
      are consistent.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|<verbatim|\\mathrm{puff}(t)> \<rightarrow\>
    <verbatim|R_{\\rm supp}(t)=4(R+2)>> (trace-local-charge.tex:766).

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>Before #477, <verbatim|puff> was used without definition. The PR
      adds one.

      <item>It agrees with Lean <verbatim|YM.Trace.Zn.puff x := 4*(rim
      x+1+1)> (<verbatim|Lean/YM/TraceExtension/Zn/Setup.lean:13>) with
      <verbatim|rim x = 1000*radiusB x> (<verbatim|Lean/YM/TraceExtension/Zn/Have.lean:59>).
      The manuscript's <math|R=1000<around|\<lceil\>|log<rsup|2>
      t|\<rceil\>>> (l.433) plays the role of <verbatim|rim>. I did not check
      <verbatim|radiusB> itself.

      <item><verbatim|countertermBound(t)=t^{-30}> (formerly
      <verbatim|calorie>) agrees with Lean <verbatim|calorie x =
      (x^30)\<#207B\>\<onesuperior\>> (same file, l.21).
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|<verbatim|\\operatorname{price}> \<rightarrow\>
    <verbatim|\\Phi>> (stationary-action-prices.tex:145\U147).

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>The PR adds the definition "half the squared chord to the
      identity". The bound <math|\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>*<around|(|C<rsub|3>*r|)><rsup|2>>
      in the next display needs exactly this definition.

      <item><verbatim|\\Phi> does not occur elsewhere in that file.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|Merge <verbatim|CTax>, <verbatim|circleTax> \<rightarrow\>
    <verbatim|\\operatorname{Cauchy}>.> Both have the same recursion:
    <math|X<around|(|R,0|)>=1> and <math|X<around|(|R,k+1|)>=X<around|(|R,k|)>/<around|(|R/2<rsup|k+1>|)>>
    (wilson-vacuum-price.tex:29\U30 and soft-curvature-receipts.tex:75\U76 in
    the old tree). The merge is legitimate.

    <item><strong|Grade dilation.>

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>The definition in sec:ex-rg-terminology is
      <math|<around|(|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|d,L>v|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=L<rsup|d-deg
      \<alpha\>>*v<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. It matches the old <verbatim|D_L> in
      ope-filtration.tex:17 and the old description of
      <verbatim|\\operatorname{vowel}_L>.

      <item>At every converted use I checked that <verbatim|d> is the grade
      in scope:

      <\itemize-minus>
        <item>centered-freight-producer.tex:7 ("degree between <math|0> and
        <math|d>");

        <item>actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex:17 and :71
        (<math|L<rsup|d-r<rsub|j>>>, as before);

        <item>heading-history-handoff.tex:376;

        <item>phase-quantitative-producer.tex:14 and :94;

        <item>selected-centered-service.tex:2046 and :2645;

        <item>ope-filtration.tex:17.
      </itemize-minus>

      <item>The rewrite <verbatim|\\operatorname{vowel}_m> \<rightarrow\>
      <verbatim|\\mathsf D^{\\rm gr}_{d,\\ell_m}> agrees with the outline's
      convention "for a step with block side <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>".
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|Price and bill operators.> Old operator names containing
    <verbatim|price> or <verbatim|bill> (76 in math mode) are all gone from
    math mode in the new tree.

    <item><strong|Rewritten sentence at centered-freight-producer.tex:29.>
    "Equal price <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>*u/\<kappa\>> and bill <math|2*u>"
    became "bounds <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>*u/\<kappa\>>,
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>*u/\<kappa\>> and <math|2*u>". These are the same
    three fields.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Findings><label|findings>

  <subsection|Required (notation)><label|required-notation>

  <strong|S1. The renames introduce symbol overloads in the same passage.>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|File
  (new tree)>>|<cell|<strong|New use>>|<cell|<strong|Pre-existing
  use>>>|<row|<cell|trace-coffer-supplier.tex:649, 694,
  703>|<cell|<verbatim|\\omega(w,s)> (weight, formerly
  <verbatim|toll>)>|<cell|<verbatim|\\omega_{a,P}(U)> singleton box
  amplitudes, l.607\U620, same subsection>>|<row|<cell|fullmesh-correlation-cauchy.tex:215>|<cell|<verbatim|\\omega_w(S)>
  (weight)>|<cell|<verbatim|\\omega_{N,m,i}> forced profile,
  l.33\U158>>|<row|<cell|stationary-output-construction.tex:327,
  399>|<cell|<verbatim|\\mathcal B_{\\rm row}> (row
  bound)>|<cell|<verbatim|\\mathcal B_{t_k}> completed determinant
  correction, l.387>>|<row|<cell|rg-construction.tex:181, 221, 224,
  325\U326>|<cell|<verbatim|\\mathcal N_r> (weighted
  norm)>|<cell|<verbatim|\\mathcal N_i> set of named origins, l.297\U298>>>>>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The first row is the most confusing. In that subsection
    \\eqref{eq:ex-trace-spiker} reads <math|<around|\||M|\|>*<space|0.17em>\<omega\><around|(|w,\<frak-s\>|)>>
    a few lines after <math|<big|sum><rsub|P>w<rsub|a><around|\||\<omega\><rsub|a,P><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>.

    <item>In fullmesh-correlation-cauchy.tex, l.329 says "identify all of the
    <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|\<omega\>> above". This now also covers the
    weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|w>>, which is not a local-section datum.

    <item>Proposed fix: use a letter for the marker-weight product that is
    not otherwise used in these files, for example <math|w<rsup|S>> or
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|w><around|(|S|)>>. Use a different letter either for
    the row bound or for the determinant correction, and either for the norm
    or for the origin sets.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|S2. The global index does not record the new symbol families.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>symbolic-index.tex lists <verbatim|\\mathcal N_d> (l.43\U45) as the
    null part of classical jets.

    <item>The PR makes <verbatim|\\mathcal N_w> and <verbatim|\\mathcal N_r>
    weighted coefficient norms in centered-construction.tex,
    centered-production.tex, centered-public-service.tex, rg-construction.tex
    and rg-multisource.tex. It also introduces the <verbatim|\\mathcal
    B_{\\rm \<ldots\>}> bound family and <verbatim|\\mathfrak M>. None of
    these is indexed.

    <item>Proposed fix: add index entries, or choose letters that are not
    already indexed.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|S3. The grade-dilation conversion is incomplete.> The PR states
  that it keeps "the grade and block size explicit at every use of the
  dilation", but two places still use other names for this dilation:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>reference-cone.tex:16\U22 defines and uses a bare <verbatim|D_L>,
    described as multiplying "the coordinate of degree <math|j\<leqslant\>d>
    by <math|L<rsup|d-j>>". This is <math|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|d,L>>.

    <item>primary-calibration-producer.tex:218 calls it <verbatim|\\mathscr
    V_L>, described as "diagonal multiplication by
    <math|L<rsup|d-g<rsub|i>>>". The neighbouring <verbatim|\\mathscr D_L>
    multiplies by <math|L<rsup|g<rsub|i>>>, which is a different map.
  </itemize-minus>

  In addition:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><verbatim|\\mathsf D_a> (annulus-fleet-face-estimates.tex:702\U706,
    a fixed direction-selection matrix) is a third <verbatim|\\mathsf D>.

    <item>symbolic-index.tex:53\U56 names only
    <math|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|d,L>> and
    <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|m>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <subsection|Non-blocking (editorial)><label|non-blocking-editorial>

  <strong|N1. Old terms remain in prose after their definitions were
  renamed.> The following now refer to objects that no longer carry those
  names:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>"toll": centered-production.tex:101, 104;
    centered-public-service.tex:58; centered-construction.tex:253;
    fullmesh-correlation-cauchy.tex:192. The object is now
    <verbatim|\\omega_w>.

    <item>"chip factors": free-kernel-estimates.tex:361, 739. The object is
    now <verbatim|\\delta_{\\rm F}>.

    <item>"mouth identity": free-kernel-estimates.tex:456, 575. The object is
    now <verbatim|landMoment>.

    <item>"rail" and "plank": kernel-transport-identities.tex:243\U244. The
    objects are now block and centered averages.

    <item>"stain/contact bound": selected-centered-service.tex:1912.

    <item>"escrow": actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex:547.
  </itemize-minus>

  Old words also survive in large numbers elsewhere, for example
  <verbatim|ticket> 125, <verbatim|receipt(s)> 465, <verbatim|invoice> 34,
  <verbatim|treasury> 29, <verbatim|palette> 48 and <verbatim|scribe> 105.
  The PR says some specialised vocabulary is intentionally kept, so I list
  only the cases where the definition itself was renamed.

  <strong|N2. Newly duplicated words and misspellings.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex:393\U394, "transfer witness
    / witness".

    <item>actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex:400 and
    selected-centered-service.tex:3028, "decompositiones".

    <item>actual-palette-warning.tex:231\U232, "bound / bound".

    <item>trace-small-means.tex:7\U8, "local bound / bound".

    <item>trace-firesky-gallery.tex:1083\U1084, "quantitative /
    quantitative".
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|N3. Garbled text from mechanical replacement.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>free-kernel-components.tex:33\U34 reads "the uniform block and
    centered averages lower and principal-centered average constants". The
    original was "rail/plank lower and principal-plank constants".

    <item>trace-clock-cap.tex:2 has the heading "The zoom bound and the
    logarithmic zoom bound". The original was "The tilt cap and the
    logarithmic zoom bound".
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Not checked><label|not-checked>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Prose substitutions that do not touch math tokens, beyond those
    read for N1\UN3. Examples are <verbatim|same>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|common>
    (34), <verbatim|history>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|trajectory> (33) and
    <verbatim|stored>\<rightarrow\><verbatim|fixed> (31). I read a sample,
    not every occurrence.

    <item>Whether every surviving occurrence of <verbatim|bound>,
    <verbatim|estimate> or <verbatim|fixed> remains unambiguous now that
    these words also replace <verbatim|price>, <verbatim|receipt> and
    <verbatim|stored>.

    <item>The LaTeX build. This report changes no <verbatim|.tex> file. The
    PR author reports a clean build; I did not rebuild the PDF or look at the
    rendered pages.

    <item>Lean: no Lean command was run. The three Lean comparisons above are
    textual readings of the pinned source.

    <item>Changes merged before #477, including #471.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Acceptance><label|acceptance>

  This is an agent report and supplies no acceptance. A responsible human
  reviewer decides whether S1\US3 must be fixed before the terminology pass
  is accepted.

